          <a href="#warranty" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Reading the Warranty, Not the Headline</a>
          <a href="#who-should-buy" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Who Should Buy Which</a>
          <a href="#buying-tips" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Pricing, Sales and Delivery</a>
          <a href="#final-word" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. The Verdict, Restated</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">12. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          "NordicTrack or ProForm?" sounds like a choice between two competitors. It is really a
          choice between two price ladders built by the same company, running the same software,
          and aimed at slightly different buyers. Once you see it that way, the decision gets
          simpler — and it usually comes down to two specific models rather than two brands.
        </p>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. NordicTrack vs ProForm: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy NordicTrack if you want the sturdier frame, the bigger screen and the wider
            incline and decline range, and you will use them. Buy ProForm if you want the same iFIT
            experience for less money and your use is moderate</strong> — walking, jogging and
            regular but not punishing running.
          </p>
          <p>
            Both brands are owned by iFIT Health &amp; Fitness, and both run the same iFIT software
            on their touchscreen models. NordicTrack is positioned as the premium line: on its
            flagship machines you generally get heavier frames, larger screens and steeper incline
            and decline ranges. ProForm is positioned as the value line: similar features at lower
            prices, achieved mostly through lighter-duty frames and motors.
          </p>
          <p>
            The important qualification is that the two price ladders overlap. A NordicTrack near the
            bottom of its range and a ProForm near the top of its range can cost roughly the same,
            and at that point the badge tells you very little. Compare the two machines on motor
            rating, deck size, weight capacity, warranty and screen, and buy the one that wins on
            the factors you care about — which is sometimes the ProForm.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="same-company" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Same Company, Same Software, Different Jobs</h2>
<p>
            iFIT Health &amp; Fitness — formerly ICON Health &amp; Fitness — owns both brands, along
            with several others. That makes NordicTrack and ProForm corporate siblings rather than
            rivals, and it explains why their machines so often look alike: shared engineering,
            shared suppliers and, on the connected models, a shared software platform.
          </p>
          <p>
            Running two badges is a familiar strategy. It lets one company cover a wider spread of
            prices without diluting the premium name. NordicTrack anchors the top end and carries
            the flagship features; ProForm captures shoppers who want touchscreen coaching but have
            a firmer budget. You see the same thing with car groups that sell a mainstream brand and
            a premium one off related platforms.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical consequence for a buyer is that you should not expect radical differences
            in philosophy, console layout or software. The differences that exist are mostly about
            how much steel, motor and screen you get for the money, which is exactly the kind of
            difference you can check on a specification sheet. Our
            <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack brand guide</a>
            and
            <a href="/proform-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm brand guide</a>
            walk through each current lineup tier by tier if you want the detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-they-differ" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Where the Brands Genuinely Differ</h2>
<p>
            Stripped of marketing, the differences cluster in five places. None of them is
            absolute — they describe the general positioning of each line, and individual models
            can buck the pattern.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Frame and weight.</strong> NordicTrack's upper tiers are generally built on
            heavier, stiffer frames. Mass and rigidity are what make a treadmill feel planted at
            running pace and what stop it creeping or flexing under a heavier user. ProForm frames
            are real steel frames rather than flimsy ones, but they are typically engineered for a
            lighter duty cycle.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Screens.</strong> The largest touchscreens in the group tend to appear on
            NordicTrack's flagships. ProForm's screens are usually smaller at a given price, and some
            ProForm models rely on a tablet holder and your own device instead. If you will follow
            video classes daily, screen size is a legitimate reason to pay more; if you will mostly
            watch your own content, it is not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline and decline range.</strong> NordicTrack's flagship lines, and in
            particular its dedicated incline trainers, reach steeper grades and decline further than
            anything in the ProForm range. For hikers and anyone who trains on hills, that is the
            single biggest functional difference between the brands. Our
            <a href="/incline-trainer-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline trainer vs treadmill</a>
            comparison covers whether you need that range.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor rating.</strong> At the top of each line, NordicTrack generally specifies
            stronger continuous-duty motors. Lower down, the gap narrows considerably.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Price.</strong> ProForm generally sits a few hundred dollars below a comparable
            NordicTrack at each tier. That is the whole point of the brand, and for many households
            it is money better spent elsewhere.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ifit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. iFIT Is the Same on Both</h2>
<p>
            On touchscreen models from either brand, the software is iFIT: the same trainer-led
            classes, the same scenic routes, and the same feature where the machine adjusts speed
            and incline automatically to follow a workout. The experience you get from that software
            is shaped mainly by the hardware it runs on — a larger screen is more immersive, and a
            wider incline range means the machine can follow steeper terrain — rather than by the
            badge.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two consequences follow. First, you cannot use the software as a tie-breaker between the
            brands; it is a wash. Second, the subscription question applies equally to both. Many
            connected models from either brand ship with a trial or an introductory membership, and
            the most distinctive features depend on keeping it. If you do not want an ongoing fee,
            check what the console does without one before you buy. Our assessment of
            <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether iFIT is worth it</a>
            covers the honest version of that trade-off.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you already know you will never subscribe, it is worth asking whether either brand is
            the right choice. A machine built around a simple console puts more of its price into the
            motor and frame; we keep a list of the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            for exactly that buyer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="overlap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The Mid-Price Overlap: Where the Badge Stops Mattering</h2>
<p>
            Picture the two ranges as ladders standing side by side, with ProForm's ladder starting
            and finishing lower. Over the bottom rungs there is no contest — ProForm is cheaper.
            Over the top rungs there is no contest either — NordicTrack's flagships and incline
            trainers do things nothing in the ProForm range does. The interesting part is the middle,
            where the top of ProForm's ladder overlaps the lower-middle of NordicTrack's.
          </p>
          <p>
            In that zone you will regularly find a ProForm and a NordicTrack at similar prices, and
            the ProForm is sometimes the better-specified machine, because it is the best thing its
            brand makes while the NordicTrack is near the bottom of its brand's range. A flagship
            from the value line can beat an entry model from the premium line on motor, deck or
            screen. Brand reputation, at that point, is a poor proxy for what you are actually
            buying.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is also where most buyers are shopping. If your budget is in the middle of the
            market, the useful question is not "which brand?" but "which of these two specific
            machines has the better motor, the longer deck, the higher weight capacity and the
            better warranty for my money?" That is a question with a checkable answer, and the next
            section is how to answer it. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-much-should-you-spend-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much to spend on a treadmill</a>
            helps decide which rung of either ladder you need in the first place.
          </p>
          <p>
            The same logic applies within NordicTrack's own range. Our comparison of the <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1250-vs-1750/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack Commercial 1250 vs. 1750</a> sets out what is confirmed to differ between two neighbouring models and what sources disagree on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. How to Compare Two Specific Models</h2>
<p>
            When you have narrowed it down to one NordicTrack and one ProForm, put their listings side
            by side and work through these in order. The order matters: the first items decide how
            long the machine lasts and whether it suits you; the last ones decide how pleasant it is.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Continuous horsepower (CHP).</strong> The continuous rating, not the peak
            figure. More continuous headroom means the motor runs cooler at your speed and under your
            weight. Runners and heavier users should weight this heavily.</li>
            <li><strong>Deck length and width.</strong> Running needs a longer deck than walking, and
            tall users need more again. A model that is cheaper because its deck is shorter is not a
            bargain if you run.</li>
            <li><strong>Maximum user weight.</strong> A higher capacity usually signals a stiffer frame
            and stronger rollers, even if you are nowhere near the limit.</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty, line by line.</strong> Frame, motor, parts and labour are separate
            terms. Compare each one, not the headline.</li>
            <li><strong>Incline and decline range.</strong> Only matters if you will use it, but it is
            the area where the flagships pull furthest apart.</li>
            <li><strong>Screen.</strong> Size, and whether it is built in or a tablet holder.</li>
            <li><strong>Folding.</strong> Convenient, but a hinge adds a joint to the frame; see our
            <a href="/folding-vs-non-folding-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding vs non-folding comparison</a>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If two machines tie on the first four items, buy the cheaper one. If they differ on the
            first two, that difference outweighs anything further down the list.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-and-deck" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Motor and Deck: The Two Numbers That Decide Longevity</h2>
<p>
            Most of the long-term difference between a value treadmill and a premium one lives in the
            motor and the deck, and both brands publish enough information to compare them.
          </p>
          <p>
            A treadmill motor is a heat problem. The harder it works relative to its continuous
            rating, the hotter it runs, and heat is what ages motors and the controller boards that
            drive them. A heavier user running at speed asks much more of a motor than a lighter user
            walking, so the same machine can be comfortably over-specified for one household and
            working at its limit in another. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            sets out how much continuous rating different uses call for.
          </p>
          <p>
            The deck matters for fit and for comfort. A longer, wider running surface gives a runner
            room to stride without thinking about the edges or the motor hood, and the
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            explains which lengths suit walking, jogging and running at different heights. Cushioning
            systems differ between models too, but they are harder to compare from a listing; deck
            size is the reliable number.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where a ProForm and a NordicTrack match on both, the cheaper one is usually the better
            buy for moderate use. Where the NordicTrack is clearly stronger on both and you run or
            are a heavier user, the extra money buys something real.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Reading the Warranty, Not the Headline</h2>
<p>
            Treadmill warranties are written as several separate promises, and the headline figure is
            almost always the longest one — usually the frame, which is the part least likely to
            fail. The terms that matter in practice are the motor, the parts and, above all, labour,
            because a free part is of limited use if you are paying for the visit to fit it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Between these two brands, warranty terms are often similar on paper at comparable price
            points, and they can change between model years. Do not assume one brand is always more
            generous. Read the warranty document for each exact model, and note three things: how
            long labour is covered, whether in-home service is included, and whether any extended or
            upgraded cover depends on registering the machine or holding a membership.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is also worth registering whichever machine you buy as soon as it arrives. Owners of
            both brands report that support is busiest in January, and having the machine registered
            with a proof of purchase on file makes any claim faster. If you are weighing a used
            machine from either brand instead, bear in mind that warranty cover often does not
            transfer to a second owner; our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new vs used guide</a>
            covers what that changes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Buy Which</h2>
<p>
            <strong>NordicTrack makes more sense if:</strong> you run regularly or plan to; you are a
            heavier user who wants a margin of frame and motor capacity; you want steep incline or
            real decline for hill and hiking training; you will follow iFIT classes daily and value a
            large screen; or you are shopping at the top of the market where ProForm has no direct
            equivalent.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>ProForm makes more sense if:</strong> your use is walking, jogging or moderate
            running a few times a week; you want the iFIT experience at the lowest price that still
            delivers a full-size deck; you are comparing in the middle of the market and the ProForm
            matches or beats the NordicTrack on motor and deck; or you would rather put the price
            difference toward a membership, a mat or simply keep it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Neither, possibly, if:</strong> you want no subscription at all, or you want the
            heaviest frame per dollar. Brands such as
            <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole</a>
            put their money into hardware and simple consoles instead of connected content, and that
            trade is the right one for a meaningful number of buyers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-tips" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Pricing, Sales and Delivery</h2>
<p>
            Both brands are discounted frequently, so the list price is rarely what you pay and the
            gap between them moves from week to week. A NordicTrack on sale can land at the same price
            as a full-price ProForm with a similar specification, which is another reason to compare
            machines rather than badges. Seasonal patterns are predictable; our guide to the
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best time of year to buy</a>
            sets them out.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check what the price includes. Delivery, in-home assembly and any bundled membership vary
            by retailer and promotion, and a cheaper headline price can be overtaken by an assembly
            charge. These are heavy machines, and upper-tier models in particular are a two-person
            job to assemble at minimum.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, measure before you buy. Larger decks and steeper incline ranges bring a larger
            footprint and, on incline-capable machines, a taller profile at full grade. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space requirements guide</a>
            covers floor area, clearance behind the deck and ceiling height.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="final-word" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. The Verdict, Restated</h2>
<p>
            <strong>NordicTrack and ProForm are two price ladders from one company.</strong> The
            software is the same, the design language is similar, and the differences are about how
            much frame, motor, screen and incline range you get for the money.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>At the extremes the brand tells you a lot</strong>: ProForm owns the low end on
            price, and NordicTrack owns the top end on capability, including the steepest incline
            machines. <strong>In the middle it tells you very little</strong>, and you should compare
            the two specific models on continuous horsepower, deck size, weight capacity and warranty
            before you look at anything else.
          </p>
          <p>
            Pay for NordicTrack's extra hardware if you will use it — running, heavier use, serious
            incline. Otherwise, ProForm's lower price is not a compromise so much as an accurate
            match to what most households actually do on a treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is NordicTrack better than ProForm?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At the top of each range, yes — NordicTrack's flagships generally have heavier frames, larger screens and wider incline and decline ranges. In the middle of the market the two overlap, and a top ProForm can match or beat an entry NordicTrack at a similar price. Compare the two specific models rather than the brands.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are NordicTrack and ProForm the same company?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Both are owned by iFIT Health &amp; Fitness, formerly ICON Health &amp; Fitness. They share engineering resources and, on touchscreen models, the same iFIT software. NordicTrack is positioned as the premium line and ProForm as the value line.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do NordicTrack and ProForm use the same iFIT app?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Touchscreen models from both brands run iFIT, with the same classes and the same automatic speed and incline control. What differs is the hardware it runs on — screen size and incline range shape the experience more than the badge does. The subscription question applies equally to both.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is ProForm cheaper than NordicTrack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mostly lighter-duty hardware: frames and motors engineered for moderate household use rather than heavy, frequent running, plus generally smaller screens and narrower incline ranges. That is a deliberate positioning choice, not a sign of poor quality, and for walking and moderate running it is often a sensible trade.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for running, NordicTrack or ProForm?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For regular or heavier running, NordicTrack's upper tiers are generally the safer bet because of their stronger continuous-duty motors and stiffer frames. For occasional running, a well-specified ProForm with a full-length deck and adequate continuous horsepower is usually fine. Check deck length and CHP on the exact model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the NordicTrack warranty better than ProForm?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not reliably. At comparable price points the terms are often similar on paper, and they change between model years. Read the warranty for each exact model, comparing motor, parts and labour separately rather than the headline frame figure, and check whether in-home service is included.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Comparing NordicTrack and ProForm</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most bad decisions here come from treating the badge as the specification. These four are the usual culprits.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming NordicTrack always means better hardware</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It does at the top of the range. In the middle, an entry NordicTrack can be outspecified by a top ProForm at a similar price. The brand describes where a model sits on its own ladder, not how it compares with the other one.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing on screen size alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The screen is the most visible difference and the least important for longevity. A bigger display on a weaker motor and shorter deck is a poor trade for a runner. Settle motor, deck and weight capacity first, then let the screen break a tie.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the subscription in the running cost</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Both brands run iFIT and both lean on it for their most distinctive features. If you will not keep a membership, check what the console does without one, and consider whether a subscription-free machine puts more of your money into hardware.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing headline warranties</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The longest term is almost always the frame, which rarely fails. Motor, parts and especially labour are what you will actually claim on. Compare each line for the exact models, and register the machine as soon as it arrives.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
